    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The viscosity of bitumen, you see, plays a crucial role in its ability to withstand thermal expansion and contraction. When bitumen is heated, its viscosity decreases, allowing it to flow more easily and fill in the cracks that form as the pavement expands. Conversely, when cooled, its viscosity increases, making it more difficult to flow and seal the cracks. This is akin to a goblin's ability to adapt to changing circumstances – when the situation is hot, the goblin can flow and navigate through tight spaces, but when the situation cools, it becomes more rigid and inflexible.\n\nIn the context of asphalt pavement, a lower viscosity bitumen can lead to a higher likelihood of crack propagation, as it is less able to fill in the cracks that form during thermal expansion. On the other hand, a higher viscosity bitumen can provide better resistance to crack propagation, as it is more able to seal the cracks that form during cooling. However, it's worth noting that excessively high viscosities can also lead to reduced workability and increased energy requirements during paving, much like how a goblin's excessive rigidity can make it more difficult to navigate through tight spaces.\n\nIn terms of specific values, a bitumen with a lower viscosity (e",
